7 \langnp2057 \langfe4105 \langfenp4105 ).}{\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af5 \loch \f5 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe4105 \langfenp4105 \par }\pard \plain \li0 \ri0 \sb100 \sa100 \sbauto1 \saauto1 \widctlpar \nooverflow \aspalpha \aspnum \faauto \adjustright \lin0 \rin0 \tql \tx6667 \tql \tx7387 \itap0 \fs20 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af0 \loch \af5 \lang1033 \langnp1033 \langfe1033 \langfenp1033 {\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af5 \loch \f5 \lang2057 \langnp2057 \langfe4105 \langfenp4105 Ovid\uc1 \u8217 ?s text sometimes creates minor confusion with names either because he does not use a specific name (or uses it very sparingly) or because he identifies someone with a phrase which is not always immediately clear to the modern reader (e.g., \uc1 \u8220 ?girl from\uc1 \u160 ?Arcady,\uc1 \u8221 ? \uc1 \u8220 ?descendant of\uc1 \u160 ?Abas,\uc1 \u8221 ? \uc1 \u8220 ?Cyllenean\uc1 \u160 ?god,\uc1 \u8221 ? and so on). In many cases, I have inserted the more familiar name (e.g.,\uc1 \u160 ?Perseus, Mercury,\uc1 \u160 ?Callisto), sometimes i addition to the original phrase, sometimes in place of it.}{\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af5 \loch \f5 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe4105 \langfenp4105 \par }\pard \plain \li0 \ri0 \sb100 \sa100 \sbauto1 \saauto1 \widctlpar \nooverflow \aspalpha \aspnum \faauto \adjustright \lin0 \rin0 \tql \tx6667 \tql \tx7387 \itap0 \fs20 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af0 \loch \af5 \lang1033 \langnp1033 \langfe1033 \langfenp1033 {\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af5 \loch \f5 \lang2057 \langnp2057 \langfe4105 \langfenp4105 Another source of minor confusion is Ovid\uc1 \u8217 ?s habit of changing verb tenses frequently from present to past and back again, often in mid-sentence. While this stylistic habit is not uncommon in conversational English, it is rare in formal English. Different translators handle this feature in different ways. Some put all verbs into the past tense, while others follow Ovid\uc1 \u8217 ?s changes faithfully. Most recent translations (so far as I can tell) retain the movement back and forth between present and past tenses, but do so less frequently than Ovid does, so that there is more consistency within short passages of the English. This last-mentioned practice is the one I have followed in this translation.}{\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af5 \loch \f5 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe4105 \langfenp4105 \par }\pard \plain \li0 \ri0 \sb100 \sa100 \sbauto1 \saauto1 \widctlpar \nooverflow \aspalpha \aspnum \faauto \adjustright \lin0 \rin0 \tql \tx6667 \tql \tx7387 \itap0 \fs20 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af0 \loch \af5 \lang1033 \langnp1033 \langfe1033 \langfenp1033 {\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af5 \loch \f5 \lang2057 \langnp2057 \langfe4105 \langfenp4105 Finally, Ovid\uc1 \u8217 ?s speeches are sometimes difficult to keep track of because he will have a speaker telling us what someone else said, and that account may include more direct speech also containing direct speech. At one point, he has speeches within a speech within a speech within a speech. To avoid complex, awkward, and confusing punctuation, I have tried to stick to a simple use of quotation marks (double quotation marks for direct speech, and single quotation marks for all speeches within speeches) and have indented the left margin appropriately to indicate how direct or indirect a particular speech is.}{\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af5 \loch \f5 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe4105 \langfenp4105 \par }\pard \plain \li0 \ri0 \sb100 \sa100 \sbauto1 \saauto1 \widctlpar \nooverflow \aspalpha \aspnum \faauto \adjustright \lin0 \rin0 \tql \tx6667 \tql \tx7387 \itap0 \fs20 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af0 \loch \af5 \lang1033 \langnp1033 \langfe1033 \langfenp1033 {\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af5 \loch \f5 \lang2057 \langnp2057 \langfe4105 \langfenp4105 I would like to acknowledge the great help I have received from other translations and commentaries, above all those by Mary M.\uc1 \u160 ?Innes, A. S. Kline, Henry T. Riley, and A. D. Melville.}{\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af5 \loch \f5 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe4105 \langfenp4105 \par }\pard \plain \li2160 \ri2160 \sb100 \sa100 \sbauto1 \saauto1 \widctlpar \nooverflow \aspalpha \aspnum \faauto \adjustright \lin2160 \rin2160 \tql \tx6667 \tql \tx7387 \itap0 \fs20 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af0 \loch \af5 \lang1033 \langnp1033 \langfe1033 \langfenp1033 {\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \loch \af0 \hich \af0 \dbch \f10 \lang1033 \langnp1033 \langfe1033 \langfenp1033 \scaps1 \sect }\sectd \sbkpage \pgwsxn12240 \pghsxn15840 \marglsxn1440 \margrsxn1440 \margtsxn1440 \margbsxn1440 \guttersxn0 \headery720 \footery720 \pgbrdropt0 \sectlinegrid360 \sectdefaultcl \sftnrstpg \endnhere {\header \pard \plain \s45 \qc \li0 \ri0 \sa120 \widctlpar \nooverflow \faroman \lin0 \rin0 \tqc \tx4320 \tqr \tx8640 \itap0 \fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af0 \loch \af5 \lang1033 \langnp1033 \langfe1033 \langfenp1033 {\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af5 \loch \f5 \lang1033 \langnp1033 \langfe1033 \langfenp1033 \scaps1 Book One}{\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af5 \loch \f5 \lang1033 \langnp1033 \langfe1033 \langfenp1033 \scaps1 \par }}\pard \plain \s50 \qc \li0 \ri0 \sb100 \sa100 \sbauto1 \saauto1 \widctlpar \nooverflow \aspalpha \aspnum \faauto \adjustright \lin0 \rin0 \tql \tx6667 \tql \tx7387 \itap0 \fs20 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af0 \loch \af9 \lang2057 \langnp2057 \langfe1033 \langfenp1033 \cf1 {\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af10 \hich \af5 \loch \f5 \lang2057 \langnp2057 \langfe1033 \langfenp1033 \cf0 \scaps1 I}{\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \loch \af5 \hich \af5 \dbch \f10 \lang2057 \langnp2057 \langfe1033 \langfenp1033 \cf0 \scaps1 \par }\pard \plain \s50 \li0 \ri0 \sb100 \sa100 \sbauto1 \saauto1 \widctlpar \nooverflow \aspalpha \aspnum \faauto \adjustright \lin0 \rin0 \tql \tx6667 \tql \tx7387 \itap0 \fs20 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af0 \loch \af9 \lang2057 \langnp2057